- How would you extend this to multi-threaded contexts with minimal locking?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests proficiency with vectorized string search, boundary handling for streaming data, and realistic benchmarking on multi-socket servers. It assesses understanding of low-level performance optimization, SIMD instruction sets, and the practical challenges of processing large-scale data streams.

## Key Concepts
- SIMD vectorization (AVX-512 with AVX2 fallback for older hardware)
- Cross-chunk boundary handling and tail management for streaming data
- Mask extraction and index reporting for match positions
- Cache behavior analysis and prefetching strategy design
- Multi-socket NUMA awareness and memory bandwidth considerations

## Code Example
```c
#include <immintrin.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define CHUNK_SIZE 64
#define TAIL_SIZE 31

typedef struct {
    size_t* positions;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;
} MatchResults;

static inline __m512i broadcast_pattern(const unsigned char* pat, size_t pat_len) {
    __m512i v = _mm512_setzero_si512();
    for (size_t i = 0; i < pat_len && i < 64; i++) {
        v = _mm512_set1_epi8(pat[i]);
    }
    return v;
}

void search_simd(const unsigned char* data, size_t n,
                 const unsigned char* pat, size_t pat_len,
                 MatchResults* results) {
    const size_t vec_width = 64;
    unsigned char tail[TAIL_SIZE] = {0};
    size_t tail_len = 0;

    size_t i = 0;
    for (; i + vec_width <= n; i += vec_width) {
        __m512i v = _mm512_loadu_si512((__m512i*)(data + i));
        __m512i p = _mm512_set1_epi8(pat[0]);
        __m512i cmp = _mm512_cmpeq_epi8(v, p);
        uint64_t mask = _mm512_mask_reduce_u64(cmp);

        while (mask) {
            uint64_t bit = __builtin_ctzll(mask);
            size_t pos = i + bit;
            if (pos + pat_len <= n && 
                memcmp(data + pos, pat, pat_len) == 0) {
                if (results->count < results->capacity) {
                    results->positions[results->count++] = pos;
                }
            }
            mask &= mask - 1;
        }
    }

    tail_len = n - i;
    if (tail_len > 0) {
        memcpy(tail, data + i, tail_len);
    }
    for (size_t j = 0; j < pat_len && tail_len + j < CHUNK_SIZE; j++) {
        tail[tail_len + j] = pat[j];
    }

    for (size_t j = 0; j < tail_len; j++) {
        if (j + pat_len <= tail_len + pat_len &&
            memcmp(tail + j, pat, pat_len) == 0) {
            if (results->count < results->capacity) {
                results->positions[results->count++] = i + j;
            }
        }
    }
}

double benchmark_search(const unsigned char* data, size_t n,
                       const unsigned char* pat, size_t pat_len,
                       int use_simd, size_t iterations) {
    struct timespec start, end;
    MatchResults results = {
        .positions = malloc(n * sizeof(size_t)),
        .count = 0,
        .capacity = n
    };

    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &start);
    for (size_t iter = 0; iter < iterations; iter++) {
        results.count = 0;
        if (use_simd) {
            search_simd(data, n, pat, pat_len, &results);
        } else {
            for (size_t i = 0; i + pat_len <= n; i++) {
                if (memcmp(data + i, pat, pat_len) == 0) {
                    if (results.count < results.capacity) {
                        results.positions[results.count++] = i;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &end);

    free(results.positions);
    return (end.tv_sec - start.tv_sec) + 
           (end.tv_nsec - start.tv_nsec) / 1e9;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Baseline**: Scalar `memcmp`/byte-by-byte search across full dataset
2. **Test Cases**:
   - Pattern at stream start (best case for early exit)
   - Pattern at stream end (worst case, full scan)
   - Pattern distributed uniformly (average case)
   - Pattern absent (negative case)
3. **Metrics**: Throughput (GB/s), latency per match, cache miss rate
4. **Variables**: Pattern length (16/32 bytes), server topology effects

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates practical memory allocator design for high-throughput systems where NUMA locality and lock-free reclamation are critical—common requirements in databases, analytics platforms, and low-latency trading systems.

## Key Concepts

- **NUMA-aware allocation**: Assign per-thread caches bound to specific NUMA nodes; central pool maintains node-local segments to minimize cross-node memory accesses
- **Lock-free per-thread caches**: Thread-local freelists eliminate contention entirely for the fast path, with batch fetching from central pool only on cache misses
- **Hazard pointers vs epoch-based reclamation**: Hazard pointers provide precise,ABA-free reclamation with bounded memory overhead, ideal for read-heavy workloads; epoch-based reclamation offers simpler implementation but may retain memory longer
- **Cache-line alignment**: Align blocks to cache line boundaries (typically 64B) and include padding to prevent false sharing between threads
- **Batch refills and backpressure**: Fetching multiple blocks per central pool access amortizes synchronization cost; capacity limits on thread-local caches prevent unbounded memory growth

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define BLOCK_SIZE  256
#define CACHE_LINE  64
#define BATCH_SIZE  16
#define CACHE_CAP   128

typedef struct Block {
    uint64_t                    magic;
    struct Block               *next;
    unsigned char               data[BLOCK_SIZE - sizeof(struct Block *)];
} Block;

typedef struct ThreadCache {
    Block          *head;
    int             count;
    int             node_id;
} ThreadCache;

typedef struct NumaNodePool {
    Block          *head;
    _Atomic size_t  count;
    int             node_id;
} NumaNodePool;

void *alloc_block(ThreadCache *tc, NumaNodePool *pool);
void  free_block(ThreadCache *tc, NumaNodePool *pool, void *ptr);
```

## Microbenchmark Outline

1. **Workload parameters**: Number of threads, allocation rate, retention duration
2. **Metrics to measure**: 
   - Allocation latency (local vs cross-node)
   - Cycles per allocation (via `rdtsc` or performance counters)
   - Cache miss rates and NUMA remote accesses (via `numactl --hardware` or hardware counters)
   - Memory consumption over time
3. **Test scenarios**:
   - All threads on same NUMA node vs distributed across nodes
   - Varying thread-local cache sizes
   - High-contention central pool vs per-node pools
4. **Expected results**: Local allocations should show 3-10x lower latency than cross-node access, with minimal false sharing overhead.

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates understanding of locality-aware, lock-free data structure design and memory reclamation strategies under multi-core contention scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Two-tier queue architecture with per-core local buffers
- ABA problem prevention using per-slot sequence counters
- Memory reclamation via epoch-based reclamation or hazard pointers
- Cache-line alignment optimization to minimize false sharing
- Progress guarantees and fairness under high contention

## Code Example
```c
// Prototypes
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; void *ptr; } slot_t;
typedef struct {
    slot_t *slots;
    atomic_uint_fast64_t head;
    atomic_uint_fast64_t tail;
    size_t capacity;
    char padding[64 - sizeof(atomic_uint_fast64_t) * 2 - sizeof(void*) - sizeof(size_t)];
} ring_buffer_t;

// Local ring operations
bool local_enqueue(ring_buffer_t *local, ring_buffer_t *global, void *item);
bool local_dequeue(ring_buffer_t *local, void **item);

// Global ring operations
bool global_enqueue(ring_buffer_t *global, void *item);
bool global_dequeue(ring_buffer_t *global, void **item);

// Batch drain from local to global
void drain_local_to_global(ring_buffer_t *local, ring_buffer_t *global);
```

## Item Flow
1. **Fast Path**: Core enqueues to its local ring buffer using simple atomic operations
2. **Transfer**: When local buffer is full/empty or during periodic draining, batch transfers occur to/from global ring
3. **Cross-Core**: Threads from different cores interact only through the global ring

## ABA Prevention
Per-slot sequence counters increment on each write, combined with slot indices to create unique identifiers that prevent ABA during compare-and-swap operations.

## Memory Reclamation
Implement epoch-based reclamation where threads enter epochs before accessing shared data, ensuring safe retirement of nodes once all threads have advanced past the epoch where nodes were unlinked.

## Progress Guarantees
- Local operations: Wait-free (bounded by local buffer size)
- Global operations: Lock-free with exponential backoff under contention
- Fairness: Priority inheritance through drain scheduling

## Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Single-Core**: Measure local operation throughput
2. **Multi-Core**: Test with varying producer/consumer ratios
3. **Contention**: Measure performance under high concurrent access
4. **Latency**: Track operation tail latency distribution
5. **Comparison**: Benchmark against single-tier lock-free queue

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical low-level memory management with thread-safety using atomic bit ops on a compact bitmap. A beginner-friendly yet realistic task for systems work.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size block pools
- Bitmap-based tracking
- Atomic CAS and memory order
- Alignment and cache-friendly layout

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stddef.h>

typedef struct {
  alignas(64) unsigned char arena[4096];
  atomic_uint64_t used;
} Pool;

void* allocate_block(Pool* p) {
  uint64_t oldv = atomic_load_explicit(&p->used, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (1) {
    uint64_t free = ~oldv;
    if (!free) return NULL;
    int idx = __builtin_ctzll(free);
    uint64_t want = oldv | (1ull << idx);
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->used, &oldv, want))
      return (void*)(p->arena + idx * 64);
  }
}

void free_block(Pool* p, void* ptr) {
  size_t idx = ((unsigned char*)ptr - p->arena) / 64;
  uint64_t mask = ~(1ull << idx);
  atomic_fetch_and_explicit(&p->used, mask, memory_order_release);
}
``` 

